Micropolar phase-field fracture: notched beam in bending
  span 1.0 x depth 0.4, notch at x = 0.35 of depth 0.12
  E = 1e+07, nu = 0.25, N = 0.5, G_c = 5.0
  l = 0.04 (4.0 cells), dx = 0.01, 2 particles per cell per direction, 12 load steps

    l_b/h      l_b  l_b/dx   peak load  normalised    W_curv  d_notch  d_clamp  reached
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    0.000    0.000      --       2.948      1.0000         0    0.999    0.078    1.000
    0.250    0.100    10.0       6.342      2.1514     0.226    0.999    0.133    1.000
    0.375    0.150    15.0       8.552      2.9014     0.362    0.999    0.161    1.000
    0.500    0.200    20.0        10.5      3.5620     0.447    0.999    0.179    1.000

    Geometry, mesh, l and G_c are identical across every row, so a classical model
    -- having no length of its own -- returns one curve for all of them. Here the peak
    load changes by 3.56x, and the curvature share of the damage driving force
    rises from 0.0e+00 to 0.447: the couple stress carries load the
    classical beam has to shed into damage.
    Monotone in l_b: True. Two points could be a coincidence of parameters;
    a monotone sweep is the statement that l_b is what is doing the work.

